"""Reproducible planning examples, not supplier quotes or capacity benchmarks."""
from pathlib import Path
import json
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
fx=.8
cases=[]
for name,cu,gb,history_gb in [('lean',.25,10,5),('steady',1,50,20),('busier',2,100,50)]:
 for plan,rate in [('launch',.106),('scale',.222)]:
  line={'compute':cu*720*rate,'database_storage':gb*.35,'retained_history':history_gb*.20,'one_snapshot_equivalent':gb*.09}
  total=sum(line.values())
  cases.append({'workload':name,'plan':plan,'hours':720,'average_cu':cu,'database_gb':gb,'history_gb':history_gb,'snapshot_gb_month':gb,'usd_line_items':line,'usd_total':total,'gbp_total':total*fx})
staging={'compute':.25*20*.106,'database_storage':5*.35,'retained_history':2*.2,'snapshot':5*.09}
supa=[]
for name,compute,gb,pitr in [('micro_daily_backups',10,10,0),('small_with_7day_pitr',15,10,100),('medium_with_7day_pitr',60,50,100)]:
 line={'pro_plan':25,'rounded_monthly_compute_anchor':compute,'org_compute_credit':-10,'extra_database_storage':max(0,gb-8)*.125,'rounded_7day_pitr_anchor':pitr}
 total=sum(line.values());supa.append({'scenario':name,'usd_line_items':line,'usd_total':total,'gbp_total':total*fx})
assert abs(cases[0]['usd_total']-24.48)<1e-8
assert abs(cases[2]['usd_total']-102.32)<1e-8
assert abs(cases[4]['usd_total']-206.64)<1e-8
assert abs(supa[1]['usd_total']-130.25)<1e-8
out={'edition':'2.5','status':'assumed_workloads_not_actual_quotes_or_equal_capacity_benchmark','gbp_per_usd_assumption_not_live_fx':fx,'neon_cases':cases,'small_staging_usd_line_items':staging,'small_staging_usd_total':sum(staging.values()),'small_staging_gbp_total':sum(staging.values())*fx,'supabase_scenarios':supa,'exclusions':['provider_tax_fx_card_fees','auth_MAU_or_feature_addons','object_media_storage_and_transfer','additional_snapshots_history_branches_egress','independent_backup_storage_and_restore_labour','hosting_workers_email_sms_maps_support','load_test_evidence_and_migration_cost']}
(R/'database-option-results.json').write_text(json.dumps(out,indent=2)+'\n')
f=lambda x:f'£{x:,.2f}'
lines=['# Taplo · Database choice and cost breakdown','', 'Working edition 2.5 · 3 November 2026. Reproduce with python3 contracts/calculate-database-options.py. Published tariffs plus explicit workload assumptions, not actual quotes or measured capacities. GBP uses assumed £0.80/USD, not a live exchange rate; taxes/FX charges excluded.','', '## Recommendation','', '**Keep Neon Postgres as the greenfield working database choice.** Its metered compute and history/branch model fit the proposed server-owned transaction engine. This is a fit judgement, not proof Neon is always cheaper or that Supabase is unsuitable. Preserve a genuinely functioning Supabase setup where migration would add cost/risk without a measured benefit. No repository/backend migration has been performed in this task.','', 'Current Neon is broader than a bare database: it advertises managed Better Auth, object storage/functions and beta realtime. Supabase similarly bundles Postgres/auth/storage/realtime. The previous shorthand contrasting bundled Supabase with database-only Neon was incomplete. Select the specific production features/regions/maturity/contract, not only the brand. No free-beta capability is required for payment or booking correctness.','', '## What the Neon example buys','', 'Inputs: Launch compute $0.106/CU-hour; Scale $0.222/CU-hour; database $0.35/GB-month; retained restore history $0.20/GB-month; snapshot $0.09/GB-month. Assume a 720-hour month, average active capacity as shown, distinct billed history volume and one full-size snapshot-equivalent retained for the month. Sources D1–D2. More changes/history/snapshots increase cost.','', '| Assumed production workload | Average capacity | Database / billed history | Launch USD total | Launch GBP | Scale GBP |','| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |']
for i in range(0,len(cases),2):
 a,b=cases[i:i+2];lines.append(f"| {a['workload']} | {a['average_cu']} CU | {a['database_gb']} GB / {a['history_gb']} GB | ${a['usd_total']:.2f} | {f(a['gbp_total'])} | {f(b['gbp_total'])} |")
lines += ['', 'These workload labels do not mean 100/500/1,000 merchants. Query patterns, concurrency, indexes, transaction locks, background tasks, geography, retention and public traffic determine capacity. An always-active worker can prevent expected idle savings. Snapshot-equivalent and billed history are explicit assumptions, not the database size magically billed twice.','', '| Lean Launch breakdown | USD/month |','| --- | ---: |']
for k,v in cases[0]['usd_line_items'].items():lines.append(f'| {k.replace("_"," ")} | ${v:.2f} |')
lines += [f"| Total | ${cases[0]['usd_total']:.2f} ≈ {f(cases[0]['gbp_total'])} |",'', f"Illustrative staging: 0.25 CU active 20 hours + 5 GB database + 2 GB billed history + one 5 GB snapshot-equivalent = ${sum(staging.values()):.2f} ≈ {f(sum(staging.values())*fx)}/month. Persistent background jobs or more retained branches change this. Use synthetic data and no copied production secrets.", '', 'Neon Launch supports a limited restore-history window; Scale has a larger window and different support/SLA features. Choose by actual recovery/security/support needs, not just the table. The published typical-spend example is not a fixed subscription. Free allowances and promotional credits are not the production financial plan.','', '## Supabase alternative with recovery included','', 'Published Pro starts at $25/month including a $10 compute credit; compare the actual project compute and included disk. Seven-day point-in-time recovery is a separate roughly $100/month add-on and requires at least Small compute under retrieved docs. Daily backups are not equivalent to continuous point-in-time recovery. Sources D3–D5.','', '| One production project scenario | USD/month illustration | GBP |','| --- | ---: | ---: |']
for s in supa:lines.append(f"| {s['scenario'].replace('_',' ')} | ${s['usd_total']:.2f} | {f(s['gbp_total'])} |")
lines+=['','These use rounded monthly catalogue compute/PITR anchors; actual hourly billing/month length differs. They include modelled disk overage above the included 8 GB, one organisation compute credit, and no other projects/overages. The £20.20 Micro example has daily backups; the £104.20 Small example includes the illustrated PITR. Do not compare the first to Neon history and claim identical recovery. Conversely, do not claim equal CU/instance names imply equal performance or that one bundle is universally cheaper.','', 'Supabase may save integration/operating work if its existing auth/storage/realtime/RLS is actually used. Neon may suit the planned independent server-owned engine and recovery economics. Actual total cost includes auth and media, security/backups and developer maintenance—not merely a database invoice. No paid database instance per merchant is proposed; use tenant-scoped shared infrastructure with future isolation paths for evidenced needs.','', '## Production operating contract','', '- Start with bounded autoscaling on paid infrastructure and monitor actual load. A working Launch range 0.25–2 CU is an initial engineering budget to validate, not a capacity guarantee. Keep latency/recovery needs explicit; do not allow scale-to-zero to surprise time-sensitive jobs.','- Server-owned money/time/stock commands use atomic database transactions and appropriate concurrency control. Tenant IDs, server authorisation, field projections and defence-in-depth database policies prevent cross-business access. A large database service key is never shipped to the browser.','- Pool connections appropriately for selected runtime/driver; load-test constrained connections and long transactions. Index actual tenant/time/status queries. Keep photos/documents out of relational rows; store access-controlled references.','- Use a separate staging boundary and synthetic fixtures. Do not connect a preview deployment to production secrets by convenience. Version schema migrations and rehearse rollback/forward-repair.','- Working recovery targets: provider-supported point-in-time recovery for small operator mistakes and an encrypted independent backup path for wider provider/account failure. Recovery objectives differ by failure; no claim that a daily off-site copy provides five-minute recovery. Set/test actual RPO/RTO against the selected plan and data volume.','- Reconcile restored payment/refund/message intents against provider truth before replay. A database restore must not resend campaigns, charge again or resurrect a cancellation as active. Preserve idempotency/provider references and audit.','- Measure database CPU/active hours, connection wait/locks, query p95, storage/history growth, backup age, restore time and per-tenant hot spots. Alerts prompt investigation, not an automatic checkout shutdown or deletion of legitimate records.','', '## Budget and selection gates','', 'Use roughly £20 production database + £3 small staging as the lean illustrated workload, then carry separately priced independent backup/monitoring and other infrastructure. At the steady assumed workload the Launch illustration is about £82, not £20 forever. These replace/reconcile the database component inside existing infrastructure allocations; never add all tables twice to the company model. Current package prices remain unchanged.','', 'Before production: actual supported region and data-transfer terms; required backup/history/support guarantees; auth/session/member scope; object-storage scope; repository dependency inventory; realistic connected load/restore evidence; actual invoice limits/alerts. Do not buy a more expensive plan for a vendor compliance badge without understanding actual data scope, but do not skip a required plan/control to preserve an artificial cheap estimate.','', '## Current primary sources','', 'D1 https://neon.com/pricing ; D2 https://neon.com/docs/introduction/plans ; D3 https://supabase.com/pricing ; D4 https://supabase.com/docs/guides/platform/manage-your-usage/compute ; D5 https://supabase.com/docs/guides/platform/backups and https://supabase.com/docs/guides/platform/manage-your-usage/point-in-time-recovery . Checked 3 November 2026. Neon and Supabase documentation evolve; current tariff/account terms override assumptions before procurement.']
(R.parent/'DATABASE-CHOICE-AND-COSTS.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n')
print(json.dumps({'neon_launch_gbp':[c['gbp_total'] for c in cases if c['plan']=='launch'],'staging_gbp':sum(staging.values())*fx,'supabase_gbp':[s['gbp_total'] for s in supa]},indent=2))
